/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL - Ezekiel chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ezekiel", [
{n:16, name:"The Foundling Bride", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 16:60",verse:"Nevertheless I will remember my covenant with thee in the days of thy youth, and I will establish unto thee an everlasting covenant."}, facts:["Did you know Ezekiel 16 is one of the longest prophetic chapters in the Bible, with 63 verses telling Jerusalem's whole story as a parable of an abandoned infant raised into a queen (Ezekiel 16:1-14)?","Did you know the iniquity of Sodom is named here not as the sin most people assume, but as pride, fullness of bread, and idleness while the poor and needy were ignored (Ezekiel 16:49)?","Did you know that after all the judgment, God ends the chapter with an everlasting covenant, remembering His covenant with Jerusalem in the days of her youth (Ezekiel 16:60)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezekiel commanded to cause Jerusalem to know?", options:["Her abominations","Her royal genealogy","Her coming victories","Her hidden riches"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:2",verse:"Son of man, cause Jerusalem to know her abominations,", insight:"God names the sin before He heals it; being told the truth is where confession begins."},
{q:"Of what land was Jerusalem's birth and nativity?", options:["The land of Canaan","The land of Egypt","The land of Assyria","The wilderness of Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:3",verse:"And say, Thus saith the Lord GOD unto Jerusalem; Thy birth and thy nativity is of the land of Canaan; thy father was an Amorite, and thy mother an Hittite.", insight:"Her roots were pagan; grace, not pedigree, made her anything at all."},
{q:"Who were Jerusalem's father and mother in the parable?", options:["Her father was an Amorite, and her mother an Hittite","Her father was a king, and her mother a queen","Her father was an Egyptian, and her mother a Syrian","Her father was a shepherd, and her mother a weaver"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:3",verse:"And say, Thus saith the Lord GOD unto Jerusalem; Thy birth and thy nativity is of the land of Canaan; thy father was an Amorite, and thy mother an Hittite.", insight:"God chose her when she had nothing to recommend her; His love comes before our loveliness."},
{q:"What was NOT done for Jerusalem in the day she was born?", options:["Her navel was not cut, nor was she washed in water to supple her","She was wrapped in royal cloth","She was anointed with oil","She was fed with honey"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:4",verse:"And as for thy nativity, in the day thou wast born thy navel was not cut, neither wast thou washed in water to supple thee; thou wast not salted at all, nor swaddled at all.", insight:"She began as a castaway; never forget the open field where God found you."},
{q:"Where was the newborn Jerusalem cast out?", options:["In the open field","At the city gate","In the wilderness","By the river's brink"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:5",verse:"None eye pitied thee, to do any of these unto thee, to have compassion upon thee; but thou wast cast out in the open field, to the lothing of thy person, in the day that thou wast born.", insight:"No eye pitied her; but the eye of God did, and that was enough."},
{q:"What did the LORD say when He saw her polluted in her blood?", options:["Live","Die","Go","Hide"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:6",verse:"And when I passed by thee, and saw thee polluted in thine own blood, I said unto thee when thou wast in thy blood, Live; yea, I said unto thee when thou wast in thy blood, Live.", insight:"Live is God's first word to the dying; grace speaks before we can ask."},
{q:"What did the LORD spread over her to cover her nakedness?", options:["His skirt","A veil of gold","A royal robe","The wings of an eagle"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:8",verse:"Now when I passed by thee, and looked upon thee, behold, thy time was the time of love; and I spread my skirt over thee, and covered thy nakedness: yea, I sware unto thee, and entered into a covenant with thee, saith the Lord GOD, and thou becamest mine.", insight:"He covered what she could not cover; covering shame is the work of a covenant God."},
{q:"What covenant act did the LORD perform for her?", options:["He sware unto her, and entered into a covenant with her, and she became His","He made her His servant","He sent her away from His presence","He crowned her queen of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:8",verse:"Now when I passed by thee, and looked upon thee, behold, thy time was the time of love; and I spread my skirt over thee, and covered thy nakedness: yea, I sware unto thee, and entered into a covenant with thee, saith the Lord GOD, and thou becamest mine.", insight:"He swore, He covenanted, and she became His; God's promises are sealed with oaths."},
{q:"How did the LORD cleanse and refresh her?", options:["He washed her with water, and anointed her with oil","He clothed her in sackcloth","He fed her with bread alone","He taught her a new song"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:9",verse:"Then washed I thee with water; yea, I throughly washed away thy blood from thee, and I anointed thee with oil.", insight:"Washed and anointed; God cleanses before He adorns."},
{q:"With what did the LORD shoe her?", options:["Badgers' skin","Sandals of brass","Slippers of cloth","Shoes of gold"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:10",verse:"I clothed thee also with broidered work, and shod thee with badgers’ skin, and I girded thee about with fine linen, and I covered thee with silk.", insight:"Even her shoes were His provision; He attends to the smallest details of those He loves."},
{q:"On what did Jerusalem feed in her glory?", options:["Fine flour, and honey, and oil","Bread and water","Milk and figs","Manna and quails"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:13",verse:"Thus wast thou decked with gold and silver; and thy raiment was of fine linen, and silk, and broidered work; thou didst eat fine flour, and honey, and oil: and thou wast exceeding beautiful, and thou didst prosper into a kingdom.", insight:"Fine flour, honey, and oil; God's table is always richer than we deserve."},
{q:"How was her beauty made perfect?", options:["Through the comeliness which God had put upon her","By her own careful adornment","By the praise of the nations","By her natural gifts"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:14",verse:"And thy renown went forth among the heathen for thy beauty: for it was perfect through my comeliness, which I had put upon thee, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"Her beauty was borrowed glory; all our loveliness is on loan from God."},
{q:"What did Jerusalem begin to trust in?", options:["Her own beauty","God's covenant","Her renown among the heathen","Her children's strength"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:15",verse:"But thou didst trust in thine own beauty, and playedst the harlot because of thy renown, and pouredst out thy fornications on every one that passed by; his it was.", insight:"Trusting in beauty is trusting in a shadow; gifts become snares when we forget the Giver."},
{q:"What did Jerusalem make with the gold and silver God had given her?", options:["Images of men","Crowns for the poor","Vessels for the temple","Gifts for her sisters"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:17",verse:"Thou hast also taken thy fair jewels of my gold and of my silver, which I had given thee, and madest to thyself images of men, and didst commit whoredom with them,", insight:"She took God's gifts and made idols; using blessings to serve sin is the oldest theft."},
{q:"Whom did Jerusalem sacrifice to the idols to be devoured?", options:["Her sons and her daughters","Her servants","Her cattle","Her captives"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:20",verse:"Moreover thou hast taken thy sons and thy daughters, whom thou hast borne unto me, and these hast thou sacrificed unto them to be devoured. Is this of thy whoredoms a small matter,", insight:"She gave her children to idols; sin always demands what is most precious."},
{q:"To whose will did God deliver her?", options:["The daughters of the Philistines","The king of Egypt","The king of Assyria","The merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:27",verse:"Behold, therefore I have stretched out my hand over thee, and have diminished thine ordinary food, and delivered thee unto the will of them that hate thee, the daughters of the Philistines, which are ashamed of thy lewd way.", insight:"God handed her to those who hated her; when we despise mercy, He lets us taste its absence."},
{q:"How was Jerusalem contrary to other harlots?", options:["She gave gifts to her lovers, and hired them","She refused all gifts","She hid in the wilderness","She asked nothing of anyone"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:33",verse:"They give gifts to all whores: but thou givest thy gifts to all thy lovers, and hirest them, that they may come unto thee on every side for thy whoredom.", insight:"She paid for her own ruin; sin always costs the sinner more than the tempter."},
{q:"What proverb would be used against her?", options:["As is the mother, so is her daughter","As the father, so the son","Pride goes before a fall","The apple falls near the tree"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:44",verse:"Behold, every one that useth proverbs shall use this proverb against thee, saying, As is the mother, so is her daughter.", insight:"She took after her Canaanite roots; children imitate what parents model, for good or ill."},
{q:"What was the iniquity of Sodom?", options:["Pride, fullness of bread, and abundance of idleness, while the poor and needy were ignored","The worship of the moon","Trading with strangers","The building of high towers"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:49",verse:"Behold, this was the iniquity of thy sister Sodom, pride, fulness of bread, and abundance of idleness was in her and in her daughters, neither did she strengthen the hand of the poor and needy.", insight:"Sodom's ruin began with full bellies and idle hands; prosperity without mercy is a road to judgment."},
{q:"What did God promise to remember?", options:["His covenant with her in the days of her youth, and to establish an everlasting covenant","Her beauty","Her gifts to Him","Her renown among the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 16:60",verse:"Nevertheless I will remember my covenant with thee in the days of thy youth, and I will establish unto thee an everlasting covenant.", insight:"When all her history was shame, God remembered His covenant; His memory outlasts our failure."}
]},
{n:17, name:"The Two Eagles", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 17:24",verse:"And all the trees of the field shall know that I the LORD have brought down the high tree, have exalted the low tree, have dried up the green tree, and have made the dry tree to flourish: I the LORD have brought down the high tree, have exalted the low tree, have dried up the green tree, and have made the dry tree to flourish: I the LORD have spoken and have done it."}, facts:["Did you know the 'great eagle' of the riddle is explained in the same chapter as the king of Babylon, who carried Judah's king and princes away to Babylon (Ezekiel 17:3, 12)?","Did you know the second eagle is Egypt, to which Judah's last king secretly sent for horses and soldiers, breaking his oath to Babylon (Ezekiel 17:7, 15)?","Did you know the chapter ends with God's own planting, a tender twig from the high cedar set upon the mountain of Israel, a promise of a restored kingdom (Ezekiel 17:22-23)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to put forth unto the house of Israel?", options:["A riddle, and a parable","A song of victory","A list of kings","A new law"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:2",verse:"Son of man, put forth a riddle, and speak a parable unto the house of Israel;", insight:"God teaches through riddles; mystery invites the heart to seek."},
{q:"What came unto Lebanon in the riddle?", options:["A great eagle with great wings, full of feathers","A young lion","A whirlwind","A cloud of locusts"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:3",verse:"And say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; A great eagle with great wings, longwinged, full of feathers, which had divers colours, came unto Lebanon, and took the highest branch of the cedar:", insight:"Babylon comes like an eagle; empires are God's instruments before they know it."},
{q:"What did the first eagle crop off from the cedar?", options:["The top of his young twigs","The roots of the vine","The fruit of the field","The branches of the willow"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:4",verse:"He cropped off the top of his young twigs, and carried it into a land of traffick; he set it in a city of merchants.", insight:"He took the highest branch; God lets pride be cropped when nations exalt themselves."},
{q:"Where did the eagle carry the twigs?", options:["Into a land of traffick, and set it in a city of merchants","Into the wilderness","Across the sea to Tarshish","To the mountains of Seir"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:4",verse:"He cropped off the top of his young twigs, and carried it into a land of traffick; he set it in a city of merchants.", insight:"Exile to Babylon; God moves kings like pieces on His own board."},
{q:"What did the eagle plant in the fruitful field?", options:["The seed of the land, by great waters, as a willow tree","Wheat and barley","Cedars of Lebanon","Thorns and thistles"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:5",verse:"He took also of the seed of the land, and planted it in a fruitful field; he placed it by great waters, and set it as a willow tree.", insight:"A vassal king planted in good soil; God plants whom He will, where He will."},
{q:"What did the planted seed become?", options:["A spreading vine of low stature","A tall cedar","A withered shrub","A forest of oaks"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:6",verse:"And it grew, and became a spreading vine of low stature, whose branches turned toward him, and the roots thereof were under him: so it became a vine, and brought forth branches, and shot forth sprigs.", insight:"A low vine, not a high cedar; God keeps dependent kingdoms low."},
{q:"What else appeared in the riddle?", options:["Another great eagle with great wings and many feathers","A second cedar","A great river","A pillar of fire"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:7",verse:"There was also another great eagle with great wings and many feathers: and, behold, this vine did bend her roots toward him, and shot forth her branches toward him, that he might water it by the furrows of her plantation.", insight:"Egypt, the second eagle; there is always another power tempting the fearful."},
{q:"What did the vine do toward the second eagle?", options:["It bent her roots and shot her branches toward him, that he might water it","It fled into the wilderness","It withered at his coming","It hid among the rocks"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:7",verse:"There was also another great eagle with great wings and many feathers: and, behold, this vine did bend her roots toward him, and shot forth her branches toward him, that he might water it by the furrows of her plantation.", insight:"She reached for Egypt instead of keeping the covenant; divided loyalties wither."},
{q:"What does God ask about the vine?", options:["Shall it prosper? shall he not pull up the roots thereof?","Shall it bear fruit forever?","Shall it be planted again?","Shall it grow taller than the cedars?"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:9",verse:"Say thou, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Shall it prosper? shall he not pull up the roots thereof, and cut off the fruit thereof, that it wither? it shall wither in all the leaves of her spring, even without great power or many people to pluck it up by the roots thereof.", insight:"God asks, Shall it prosper? Disobedience never prospers in the end."},
{q:"What would make the vine utterly wither?", options:["The east wind touching it","The winter frost","The locusts of the field","The heat of the sun at noon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:10",verse:"Yea, behold, being planted, shall it prosper? shall it not utterly wither, when the east wind toucheth it? it shall wither in the furrows where it grew.", insight:"One east wind from God, and man's schemes dry up in the furrow."},
{q:"Who is the first eagle, as God explains it?", options:["The king of Babylon, who is come to Jerusalem","The king of Egypt","The king of Tyre","The prince of Persia"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:12",verse:"Say now to the rebellious house, Know ye not what these things mean? tell them, Behold, the king of Babylon is come to Jerusalem, and hath taken the king thereof, and the princes thereof, and led them with him to Babylon;", insight:"God decodes His own riddle; He never leaves His people guessing about judgment."},
{q:"What did the king of Babylon take of Zedekiah?", options:["A covenant, and an oath","His crown and his gold","His sons and his daughters","His armies and his chariots"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:13",verse:"And hath taken of the king’s seed, and made a covenant with him, and hath taken an oath of him: he hath also taken the mighty of the land:", insight:"An oath was taken; God takes covenants seriously, even between kings."},
{q:"Why was the kingdom kept base and low?", options:["That it might not lift itself up, but by keeping of his covenant it might stand","To punish the poor","To enrich Babylon","To build new cities"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:14",verse:"That the kingdom might be base, that it might not lift itself up, but that by keeping of his covenant it might stand.", insight:"Lowliness was Judah's safety; keeping covenant matters more than rising high."},
{q:"How did Zedekiah rebel against Babylon?", options:["By sending his ambassadors into Egypt, that they might give him horses and much people","By building an altar to Baal","By refusing to pay tribute","By fleeing to the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:15",verse:"But he rebelled against him in sending his ambassadors into Egypt, that they might give him horses and much people. Shall he prosper? shall he escape that doeth such things? or shall he break the covenant, and be delivered?", insight:"He sent to Egypt for horses; trusting in arms of flesh breaks oaths made before God."},
{q:"Where would the oath-breaking king die?", options:["In the midst of Babylon","In the wilderness","In Jerusalem","In the land of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:16",verse:"As I live, saith the Lord GOD, surely in the place where the king dwelleth that made him king, whose oath he despised, and whose covenant he brake, even with him in the midst of Babylon he shall die.", insight:"He died where his oath was made; God keeps the reckoning of broken vows."},
{q:"Would Pharaoh's mighty army save him in the war?", options:["No","Yes, with chariots","Yes, with ships","Yes, for a little season"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:17",verse:"Neither shall Pharaoh with his mighty army and great company make for him in the war, by casting up mounts, and building forts, to cut off many persons:", insight:"Egypt could not save him; no earthly power rescues the covenant breaker."},
{q:"What had he despised?", options:["The oath, by breaking the covenant, when he had given his hand","The sabbath day","The words of the prophets","The temple of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:18",verse:"Seeing he despised the oath by breaking the covenant, when, lo, he had given his hand, and hath done all these things, he shall not escape.", insight:"He gave his hand, then broke his word; God marks the difference between promising and performing."},
{q:"What would God spread upon him?", options:["His net, and take him in His snare, and bring him to Babylon","A covering of darkness","A famine upon the land","A plague upon his house"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:20",verse:"And I will spread my net upon him, and he shall be taken in my snare, and I will bring him to Babylon, and will plead with him there for his trespass that he hath trespassed against me.", insight:"God's net is wider than Babylon's; no rebel outruns the Snarer of heaven."},
{q:"What would God Himself plant?", options:["A tender one from the top of the cedar, upon a high mountain and eminent","A new vine in Egypt","A forest in the wilderness","A garden by the river"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:22",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; I will also take of the highest branch of the high cedar, and will set it; I will crop off from the top of his young twigs a tender one, and will plant it upon an high mountain and eminent:", insight:"After judgment, God plants; His last word is not exile but restoration."},
{q:"What would all the trees of the field know?", options:["That the LORD brought down the high tree, and exalted the low tree","That the cedar would never fall","That the vine would prosper","That the east wind would cease"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 17:24",verse:"And all the trees of the field shall know that I the LORD have brought down the high tree, have exalted the low tree, have dried up the green tree, and have made the dry tree to flourish: I the LORD have brought down the high tree, have exalted the low tree, have dried up the green tree, and have made the dry tree to flourish: I the LORD have spoken and have done it.", insight:"He brings down the high and lifts the low; that is the whole story of God with men."}
]},
{n:18, name:"The Soul That Sinneth", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 18:32",verse:"For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith the Lord GOD: wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye."}, facts:["Did you know Ezekiel 18:4, 'the soul that sinneth, it shall die,' is one of the most quoted single verses in all of Ezekiel?","Did you know this chapter's marks of the just man include refusing usury, restoring the debtor's pledge, feeding the hungry, and clothing the naked (Ezekiel 18:7-8)?","Did you know God asks one of Scripture's tenderest questions here: 'Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die? and not that he should return from his ways, and live?' (Ezekiel 18:23)?"], questions:[
{q:"What proverb were the people using concerning the land of Israel?", options:["The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge","A soft answer turneth away wrath","Pride goeth before destruction","The apple falleth near the tree"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:2",verse:"What mean ye, that ye use this proverb concerning the land of Israel, saying, The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children’s teeth are set on edge?", insight:"They blamed their fathers; God will not let us hide behind our parents."},
{q:"What did God say about that proverb?", options:["They would have no more occasion to use it in Israel","It would be written in the law","It would be sung in the temple","It would be taught to their children"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:3",verse:"As I live, saith the Lord GOD, ye shall not have occasion any more to use this proverb in Israel.", insight:"God retires our excuses; His word ends the blame game."},
{q:"What does God declare about souls?", options:["All souls are Mine; the soul that sinneth, it shall die","Only the fathers' souls are His","Souls belong to the priests","The soul of the son is free of all judgment"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:4",verse:"Behold, all souls are mine; as the soul of the father, so also the soul of the son is mine: the soul that sinneth, it shall die.", insight:"Every soul is His; responsibility is personal, not inherited."},
{q:"What had the just man NOT done?", options:["Eaten upon the mountains, nor lifted up his eyes to the idols of the house of Israel","Given his bread to the hungry","Restored the pledge to the debtor","Walked in God's statutes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:6",verse:"And hath not eaten upon the mountains, neither hath lifted up his eyes to the idols of the house of Israel, neither hath defiled his neighbour’s wife, neither hath come near to a menstruous woman,", insight:"Holiness begins with refusal; the just man first says no to idols."},
{q:"What had the just man restored?", options:["The pledge to the debtor","The tithe to the priest","The sword to the soldier","The land to the king"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:7",verse:"And hath not oppressed any, but hath restored to the debtor his pledge, hath spoiled none by violence, hath given his bread to the hungry, and hath covered the naked with a garment;", insight:"He restored the pledge; justice returns what was taken."},
{q:"To whom had the just man given his bread?", options:["The hungry, and he covered the naked with a garment","The priests at the temple","The king's servants","His own household alone"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:7",verse:"And hath not oppressed any, but hath restored to the debtor his pledge, hath spoiled none by violence, hath given his bread to the hungry, and hath covered the naked with a garment;", insight:"Bread to the hungry, clothing to the naked; true righteousness feeds and covers."},
{q:"What had the just man refused to do with his money?", options:["Give forth upon usury, nor take any increase","Lend to strangers","Save for his children","Pay taxes to the king"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:8",verse:"He that hath not given forth upon usury, neither hath taken any increase, that hath withdrawn his hand from iniquity, hath executed true judgment between man and man,", insight:"No usury, no increase; God forbids profit from a brother's need."},
{q:"What is promised to the just man?", options:["He shall surely live","He shall be rich","He shall be a king","He shall never suffer"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:9",verse:"Hath walked in my statutes, and hath kept my judgments, to deal truly; he is just, he shall surely live, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"He shall surely live; the promise is life, not luxury."},
{q:"What of the just man's son, if he is a robber and a shedder of blood?", options:["He shall surely die; his blood shall be upon him","He shall live for his father's sake","He shall be sent into exile","He shall pay double"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:13",verse:"Hath given forth upon usury, and hath taken increase: shall he then live? he shall not live: he hath done all these abominations; he shall surely die; his blood shall be upon him.", insight:"A father's righteousness does not cover a son's sin; each stands alone before God."},
{q:"What of the wicked man's son, if he sees his father's sins and does not do them?", options:["He shall not die for the iniquity of his father; he shall surely live","He shall die with his father","He shall be punished lightly","He shall flee to another land"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:17",verse:"That hath taken off his hand from the poor, that hath not received usury nor increase, hath executed my judgments, hath walked in my statutes; he shall not die for the iniquity of his father, he shall surely live.", insight:"A father's sin does not doom the son; grace breaks the chain."},
{q:"Who bears the iniquity of the father?", options:["The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son","The eldest son bears it","The priests bear it","The whole nation bears it"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:20",verse:"The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him.", insight:"Each bears his own; God judges persons, not pedigrees."},
{q:"What if the wicked turns from all his sins?", options:["He shall surely live, he shall not die","He shall be punished for half of them","He shall be remembered no more","He shall begin again as a child"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:21",verse:"But if the wicked will turn from all his sins that he hath committed, and keep all my statutes, and do that which is lawful and right, he shall surely live, he shall not die.", insight:"Turn, and live; repentance rewrites the ending."},
{q:"What will happen to his past transgressions?", options:["They shall not be mentioned unto him","They shall be written in a book","They shall be told to his children","They shall follow him to the grave"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:22",verse:"All his transgressions that he hath committed, they shall not be mentioned unto him: in his righteousness that he hath done he shall live.", insight:"Not mentioned; God forgives so completely that the record is wiped clean."},
{q:"Does God take pleasure in the death of the wicked?", options:["No; He desires that he should return from his ways, and live","Yes, when they are great sinners","Only when they refuse correction","He is indifferent to it"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:23",verse:"Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die? saith the Lord GOD: and not that he should return from his ways, and live?", insight:"God finds no joy in the sinner's death; His heart leans toward mercy."},
{q:"What if the righteous turns away from his righteousness?", options:["All his righteousness shall not be mentioned; in his sin he shall die","He shall be warned once","His past will save him","He shall be forgiven automatically"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:24",verse:"But when the righteous turneth away from his righteousness, and committeth iniquity, and doeth according to all the abominations that the wicked man doeth, shall he live? All his righteousness that he hath done shall not be mentioned: in his trespass that he hath trespassed, and in his sin that he hath sinned, in them shall he die.", insight:"Past righteousness does not insure present rebellion; we must finish as we began."},
{q:"What did Israel say of the way of the Lord?", options:["The way of the Lord is not equal","The way of the Lord is too hard","The way of the Lord is hidden","The way of the Lord is for priests only"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:25",verse:"Yet ye say, The way of the Lord is not equal. Hear now, O house of Israel; Is not my way equal? are not your ways unequal?", insight:"They called God unfair; the real complaint was that He was fair."},
{q:"How will God judge the house of Israel?", options:["Every one according to his ways","All together as one nation","Only the priests and kings","By the sins of their fathers"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:30",verse:"Therefore I will judge you, O house of Israel, every one according to his ways, saith the Lord GOD. Repent, and turn yourselves from all your transgressions; so iniquity shall not be your ruin.", insight:"Every one according to his ways; God's judgment is personal and precise."},
{q:"What command does God give them?", options:["Repent, and turn yourselves from all your transgressions","Offer more sacrifices","Build a new temple","Flee to the mountains"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:30",verse:"Therefore I will judge you, O house of Israel, every one according to his ways, saith the Lord GOD. Repent, and turn yourselves from all your transgressions; so iniquity shall not be your ruin.", insight:"Repent and turn; the command is short, and it is everything."},
{q:"What new things are they to make for themselves?", options:["A new heart and a new spirit","A new temple and a new altar","A new city and new walls","A new song and new garments"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:31",verse:"Cast away from you all your transgressions, whereby ye have transgressed; and make you a new heart and a new spirit: for why will ye die, O house of Israel?", insight:"A new heart and a new spirit; God asks for inward renewal, not outward repair."},
{q:"What is God's final word in the chapter?", options:["Turn yourselves, and live ye","Depart from Me","Be silent before Me","Wait for the morning"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 18:32",verse:"For I have no pleasure in the death of him that dieth, saith the Lord GOD: wherefore turn yourselves, and live ye.", insight:"Turn yourselves, and live ye; the last word of judgment is an invitation."}
]},
{n:19, name:"Lamentation for the Princes", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 19:14",verse:"And fire is gone out of a rod of her branches, which hath devoured her fruit, so that she hath no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule. This is a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation."}, facts:["Did you know the two young lions of this lamentation are widely understood as Judah's last kings: the first carried in chains to Egypt, the second to Babylon (Ezekiel 19:4, 9)?","Did you know the mother vine's 'strong rods for the sceptres of them that bare rule' picture the royal line of David, broken and burned by judgment (Ezekiel 19:11-12)?","Did you know this chapter ends by naming itself: 'This is a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation' (Ezekiel 19:14)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Ezekiel told to take up for the princes of Israel?", options:["A lamentation","A song of triumph","A new covenant","A census of the tribes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:1",verse:"Moreover take thou up a lamentation for the princes of Israel,", insight:"A lamentation, not a celebration; some chapters of God's story are written in tears."},
{q:"What was their mother likened to?", options:["A lioness","An eagle","A vine","A cedar"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:2",verse:"And say, What is thy mother? A lioness: she lay down among lions, she nourished her whelps among young lions.", insight:"Judah, the lion's tribe, is pictured as a lioness; the royal line had teeth."},
{q:"What did the first young lion learn?", options:["To catch the prey; it devoured men","To roar in the temple","To sleep in the den","To drink from the river"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:3",verse:"And she brought up one of her whelps: it became a young lion, and it learned to catch the prey; it devoured men.", insight:"He learned to devour men; power without righteousness becomes cruelty."},
{q:"Who heard of the young lion?", options:["The nations","The priests","The prophets","The merchants"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:4",verse:"The nations also heard of him; he was taken in their pit, and they brought him with chains unto the land of Egypt.", insight:"The nations heard; a wicked king's fame spreads farther than his kingdom."},
{q:"What became of the first young lion?", options:["He was taken in their pit, and brought with chains unto the land of Egypt","He fled to the wilderness","He was crowned in Jerusalem","He made peace with the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:4",verse:"The nations also heard of him; he was taken in their pit, and they brought him with chains unto the land of Egypt.", insight:"Taken in a pit, chained to Egypt; crowns do not protect the faithless."},
{q:"What did the lioness do when she saw that her hope was lost?", options:["She took another of her whelps, and made him a young lion","She fled to the mountains","She mourned for forty days","She made peace with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:5",verse:"Now when she saw that she had waited, and her hope was lost, then she took another of her whelps, and made him a young lion.", insight:"Hope lost, she tried again; nations keep raising up what God keeps removing."},
{q:"What did the second young lion learn?", options:["To catch the prey, and he devoured men","To build cities","To write laws","To tend sheep"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:6",verse:"And he went up and down among the lions, he became a young lion, and learned to catch the prey, and devoured men.", insight:"The second lion learned the same cruelty; untaught hearts repeat the same sins."},
{q:"What did the second lion do to the cities?", options:["He laid waste their cities, and the land was desolate by the noise of his roaring","He built walls around Jerusalem","He planted vineyards","He dug wells in the desert"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:7",verse:"And he knew their desolate palaces, and he laid waste their cities; and the land was desolate, and the fulness thereof, by the noise of his roaring.", insight:"His roaring made the land desolate; tyrants tax the very ground they rule."},
{q:"Who set against the second lion?", options:["The nations set against him on every side from the provinces","The priests of the temple","His own brothers","The shepherds of the field"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:8",verse:"Then the nations set against him on every side from the provinces, and spread their net over him: he was taken in their pit.", insight:"Nations on every side; when God is done with a king, the world closes in."},
{q:"What did the nations spread over him?", options:["Their net","A covering of darkness","Chains of brass","A snare of ropes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:8",verse:"Then the nations set against him on every side from the provinces, and spread their net over him: he was taken in their pit.", insight:"A net over a lion; God lets the mighty be caught by the weak."},
{q:"Where was the second young lion brought?", options:["To the king of Babylon","To the king of Egypt","To the wilderness of Sinai","To the mountains of Seir"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:9",verse:"And they put him in ward in chains, and brought him to the king of Babylon: they brought him into holds, that his voice should no more be heard upon the mountains of Israel.", insight:"Brought to Babylon; the exile began a seventy-year night."},
{q:"Why was he brought into holds?", options:["That his voice should no more be heard upon the mountains of Israel","To learn the Chaldean tongue","To serve in the palace","To be judged by the priests"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:9",verse:"And they put him in ward in chains, and brought him to the king of Babylon: they brought him into holds, that his voice should no more be heard upon the mountains of Israel.", insight:"His voice silenced on Israel's mountains; judgment can mute a king."},
{q:"What was the mother also likened to?", options:["A vine in thy blood, planted by the waters","A cedar in Lebanon","An olive tree in the field","A fig tree by the way"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:10",verse:"Thy mother is like a vine in thy blood, planted by the waters: she was fruitful and full of branches by reason of many waters.", insight:"A vine by many waters; Judah was planted in privilege."},
{q:"Why was the vine fruitful and full of branches?", options:["By reason of many waters","By the skill of the gardeners","By the richness of the soil alone","By the sun of the south"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:10",verse:"Thy mother is like a vine in thy blood, planted by the waters: she was fruitful and full of branches by reason of many waters.", insight:"Many waters made her fruitful; blessing, not merit, grew her branches."},
{q:"What were her strong rods for?", options:["Sceptres of them that bare rule","Weapons of war","Beams of the temple","Staffs of shepherds"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:11",verse:"And she had strong rods for the sceptres of them that bare rule, and her stature was exalted among the thick branches, and she appeared in her height with the multitude of her branches.", insight:"Strong rods for sceptres; God gave Judah kings from David's line."},
{q:"What happened to the vine?", options:["She was plucked up in fury, and cast down to the ground","She was pruned with care","She was watered anew","She was moved to a garden"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:12",verse:"But she was plucked up in fury, she was cast down to the ground, and the east wind dried up her fruit: her strong rods were broken and withered; the fire consumed them.", insight:"Plucked up in fury; the hand that planted can also uproot."},
{q:"What dried up her fruit?", options:["The east wind","The winter frost","A drought of three years","The locusts"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:12",verse:"But she was plucked up in fury, she was cast down to the ground, and the east wind dried up her fruit: her strong rods were broken and withered; the fire consumed them.", insight:"The east wind of Babylon dried her; God's judgments come like weather, sudden and sure."},
{q:"Where is the vine planted now?", options:["In the wilderness, in a dry and thirsty ground","In the garden of the king","By the rivers of Babylon","On the mountains of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:13",verse:"And now she is planted in the wilderness, in a dry and thirsty ground.", insight:"A vine in a dry ground; exile is fruitful soil with no water."},
{q:"What went out of a rod of her branches?", options:["Fire, which devoured her fruit","A new shoot of hope","Water for the thirsty","A dove with an olive leaf"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:14",verse:"And fire is gone out of a rod of her branches, which hath devoured her fruit, so that she hath no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule. This is a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation.", insight:"Fire from her own branches; rebellion burned what remained."},
{q:"What does the vine have no longer?", options:["No strong rod to be a sceptre to rule","No leaves for shade","No fruit for the poor","No roots in the ground"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 19:14",verse:"And fire is gone out of a rod of her branches, which hath devoured her fruit, so that she hath no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule. This is a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation.", insight:"No sceptre left; the lament ends where the kingdom ended, in silence."}
]},
{n:20, name:"For My Name's Sake", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 20:44",verse:"And ye shall know that I am the LORD, when I have wrought with you for my name’s sake, not according to your wicked ways, nor according to your corrupt doings, O ye house of Israel, saith the Lord GOD."}, facts:["Did you know Ezekiel 20 retells the whole history of Israel's rebellion, from Egypt to the wilderness to the promised land, in a single chapter (Ezekiel 20:5-32)?","Did you know the elders came to Ezekiel in the seventh year of the exile, but God refused to be enquired of by them because their hearts were still on idols (Ezekiel 20:1, 3)?","Did you know 'Bamah' means simply 'high place,' and it became such a common word for Israel's idol shrines that God makes a point of it: 'the name thereof is called Bamah unto this day' (Ezekiel 20:29)?"], questions:[
{q:"When did certain of the elders of Israel come to Ezekiel?", options:["In the seventh year, in the fifth month, on the tenth day of the month","In the first year of the exile","At the feast of trumpets","In the year of jubilee"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the seventh year, in the fifth month, the tenth day of the month, that certain of the elders of Israel came to enquire of the LORD, and sat before me.", insight:"A dated visit; God records the day men came seeking Him with divided hearts."},
{q:"Why did the elders come and sit before Ezekiel?", options:["To enquire of the LORD","To offer sacrifices","To crown a new king","To build a temple in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the seventh year, in the fifth month, the tenth day of the month, that certain of the elders of Israel came to enquire of the LORD, and sat before me.", insight:"They came to enquire; seeking God while serving idols is a hollow errand."},
{q:"What was God's answer to their enquiry?", options:["As I live, I will not be enquired of by you","Ask, and ye shall receive","Wait seven days","Bring your offerings first"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:3",verse:"Son of man, speak unto the elders of Israel, and say unto them, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Are ye come to enquire of me? As I live, saith the Lord GOD, I will not be enquired of by you.", insight:"God refused their enquiry; He does not answer hearts that have already answered Him."},
{q:"What was Ezekiel to cause the elders to know?", options:["The abominations of their fathers","The names of the exiles","The riches of Babylon","The days of the feasts"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:4",verse:"Wilt thou judge them, son of man, wilt thou judge them? cause them to know the abominations of their fathers:", insight:"Know your fathers' abominations; history ignored becomes history repeated."},
{q:"Into what kind of land had God promised to bring them out of Egypt?", options:["A land flowing with milk and honey, the glory of all lands","A land of iron and brass","A desert of hidden wells","A mountain of gold"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:6",verse:"In the day that I lifted up mine hand unto them, to bring them forth of the land of Egypt into a land that I had espied for them, flowing with milk and honey, which is the glory of all lands:", insight:"Milk, honey, and glory; God's promises are always larger than our deserving."},
{q:"What were they told to cast away in Egypt?", options:["Every man the abominations of his eyes, and the idols of Egypt","Their gold and silver","Their Egyptian clothes","Their brick-making tools"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:7",verse:"Then said I unto them, Cast ye away every man the abominations of his eyes, and defile not yourselves with the idols of Egypt: I am the LORD your God.", insight:"Cast away Egypt's idols; you cannot carry Egypt into Canaan."},
{q:"What did they do instead in Egypt?", options:["They rebelled against Him, and would not hearken unto Him","They obeyed gladly","They fled to the wilderness","They built altars to the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:8",verse:"But they rebelled against me, and would not hearken unto me: they did not every man cast away the abominations of their eyes, neither did they forsake the idols of Egypt: then I said, I will pour out my fury upon them to accomplish my anger against them in the midst of the land of Egypt.", insight:"Rebellion began in Egypt, not the wilderness; bondage does not make saints."},
{q:"Why did God not pour out His fury upon them in Egypt?", options:["For His name's sake, that it should not be polluted before the heathen","Because they repented","Because Moses pleaded","Because Pharaoh relented"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:9",verse:"But I wrought for my name’s sake, that it should not be polluted before the heathen, among whom they were, in whose sight I made myself known unto them, in bringing them forth out of the land of Egypt.", insight:"For His name's sake; again and again, God's mercy is anchored in His own glory."},
{q:"What did God give them, which if a man do, he shall even live in them?", options:["His statutes, and His judgments","Gold and silver","Cities and vineyards","Horses and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:11",verse:"And I gave them my statutes, and shewed them my judgments, which if a man do, he shall even live in them.", insight:"Statutes to live in; God's law is not a cage but a house of life."},
{q:"What did God give as a sign between Him and them?", options:["His sabbaths","The rainbow","Circumcision","The passover lamb"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:12",verse:"Moreover also I gave them my sabbaths, to be a sign between me and them, that they might know that I am the LORD that sanctify them.", insight:"The sabbath as a sign; rest is the mark of a people set apart."},
{q:"How did the house of Israel respond in the wilderness?", options:["They rebelled, walked not in His statutes, and greatly polluted His sabbaths","They built the tabernacle gladly","They kept the sabbath holy","They sang songs of deliverance"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:13",verse:"But the house of Israel rebelled against me in the wilderness: they walked not in my statutes, and they despised my judgments, which if a man do, he shall even live in them; and my sabbaths they greatly polluted: then I said, I would pour out my fury upon them in the wilderness, to consume them.", insight:"Rebellion in the wilderness; the generation that saw miracles still would not obey."},
{q:"What did God give them because of their rebellion?", options:["Statutes that were not good, and judgments whereby they should not live","New commandments","A second covenant","The laws of the heathen"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:25",verse:"Wherefore I gave them also statutes that were not good, and judgments whereby they should not live;", insight:"God gave them over to their own ways; the darkest judgment is being left to your choices."},
{q:"What is Bamah?", options:["The name of the high place whereunto they went, called so unto this day","A city in Babylon","A feast of the harvest","A song of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:29",verse:"Then I said unto them, What is the high place whereunto ye go? And the name thereof is called Bamah unto this day.", insight:"Bamah just means high place; their idolatry was so common it needed no other name."},
{q:"How would God rule over them?", options:["With a mighty hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with fury poured out","With gentleness and mercy","Through the elders","By the hand of Cyrus"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:33",verse:"As I live, saith the Lord GOD, surely with a mighty hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with fury poured out, will I rule over you:", insight:"A mighty hand and fury poured out; the God they would not have as Shepherd they would meet as Judge."},
{q:"What would God cause them to do?", options:["Pass under the rod, and bring them into the bond of the covenant","Cross the Red Sea again","Build a new temple","Return to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:37",verse:"And I will cause you to pass under the rod, and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant:", insight:"Under the rod, into the covenant; God counts His sheep and binds them to Himself."},
{q:"Whom would God purge out from among them?", options:["The rebels, and them that transgress against Him","The poor and needy","The strangers","The priests"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:38",verse:"And I will purge out from among you the rebels, and them that transgress against me: I will bring them forth out of the country where they sojourn, and they shall not enter into the land of Israel: and ye shall know that I am the LORD.", insight:"The rebels purged; not all who leave Egypt enter Canaan."},
{q:"What would happen in God's holy mountain?", options:["All the house of Israel would serve Him there, and He would accept them","A new temple would be built by strangers","The nations would be destroyed","The sabbath would be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:40",verse:"For in mine holy mountain, in the mountain of the height of Israel, saith the Lord GOD, there shall all the house of Israel, all of them in the land, serve me: there will I accept them, and there will I require your offerings, and the firstfruits of your oblations, with all your holy things.", insight:"All Israel serving on His holy mountain; restoration is the last chapter of rebellion."},
{q:"Why would God act for them in the end?", options:["For His name's sake, not according to their wicked ways","Because they deserved it","Because the elders pleaded","Because Babylon was kind"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:44",verse:"And ye shall know that I am the LORD, when I have wrought with you for my name’s sake, not according to your wicked ways, nor according to your corrupt doings, O ye house of Israel, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"For His name's sake; our restoration rests on His reputation, not our record."},
{q:"What would the fire kindled in the forest of the south devour?", options:["Every green tree and every dry tree","Only the dead branches","The cities of the plain","The idols of the high places"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:47",verse:"And say to the forest of the south, Hear the word of the LORD; Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I will kindle a fire in thee, and it shall devour every green tree in thee, and every dry tree: the flaming flame shall not be quenched, and all faces from the south to the north shall be burned therein.", insight:"Green tree and dry alike; when judgment burns, no one is exempt."},
{q:"What did Ezekiel say the people were saying about him?", options:["Doth he not speak parables?","Is he not a priest?","Will he not return to Jerusalem?","Doth he not eat unclean food?"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 20:49",verse:"Then said I, Ah Lord GOD! they say of me, Doth he not speak parables?", insight:"They called his preaching parables; when the word stings, men blame the preacher's style."}
]}
]);
